ID: B3-Ch08b-Pardon
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch6_kelrim != 1 && v_b3_ch6_plan != 3) {
 "Pardon me," the king of the underground says from my shoulder. I turn to find him right at my elbow. "I hope I am not interrupting anything vital. There are matters concerning the Overseer I would like to put before your group, and since you are all assembled, this strikes me as an opportune moment."

Torchlight flickers against bare stone. I take in our surroundings—we have marched straight into the waiting cavern while arguing, oblivious to the change in scenery. After the blast, not a single blue crystal remains embedded in the walls or ceiling, leaving the wide chamber in a dull orange haze. Rebels and refugees mill between the pillars, most of them faces I know only from passing glances in the corridors. Albert and Peter flank their leader, and near the quarantine barrier, Desmond keeps watch with his arms crossed over his chest.

"No trouble at all," Daren says. "We were looking for you as well. Do you mind if we ask a question before we get into plans for the Overseer?"

"By all means," the king says. "Ask."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_kelrim == 1 || v_b3_ch6_plan == 3) {
 "Pardon me," the king of the underground says from my shoulder. I turn to find him right at my elbow. "I hope I am not interrupting anything vital. There are matters concerning the Overseer I would like to put before your group, and since you are all assembled, this strikes me as an opportune moment."

Torchlight flickers against bare stone. I take in our surroundings—we have marched straight into the waiting cavern while arguing, oblivious to the change in scenery. After the blast, not a single blue crystal remains embedded in the walls or ceiling, leaving the wide chamber in a dull orange haze. Rebels and refugees mill between the pillars, most of them faces I know only from passing glances in the corridors. Albert and Peter flank their leader, and near the quarantine barrier, Desmond keeps watch with his arms crossed over his chest. Kelrim stands over by the far wall, deep in conversation with a wounded rebel and too preoccupied to glance our way.

"No trouble at all," Daren says. "We were looking for you as well. Do you mind if we ask a question before we get into plans for the Overseer?"

"By all means," the king says. "Ask."

}
 "We want to know if you have settled on who takes the throne of Ollendor next," Daren says.

"We have not reached a final determination," the king of the underground says, trading a brief, measured look with Albert. "The probable course is an elected council once order is restored to the city."

"Once order is restored," Daren echoes. "And until then? What happens the moment the Overseer falls?"

"A transitional phase," the king says, smooth and even. "A period requiring steady leadership to hold the population together and prevent panic. The death of the reigning monarch will already destabilize Ollendor. Upending the entire structure of government overnight would only invite chaos. The transition must be gradual. The concept of an elected council needs to be introduced by a monarch the people recognize and trust."

"And who is this trusted ruler going to be?" Kate asks, her voice cutting like frost. "You?"

"If the people demand it, I will not shirk my duty," the king says. "The choice remains open, however. You are welcome to offer candidates you believe suited to the mantle."

