ID: B3-Ch08b-King
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 0) {
 "How about Albert?"

"An intriguing suggestion," the king of the underground says, stroking his trimmed beard. "Given how often you see the two of us side by side, I would have assumed you saw little distinction between him on the throne and me."

"There is a difference," I say. "Albert serves you now, but he led rebels before he took your orders. He is far more likely to remember the people of Ollendor than you are. And since he is already your right hand, you would have a harder time objecting if the tunnels voted for him over you."

"A fair point," the king says. "Albert on the throne is a compromise I could accept, if circumstance demands it. Does your party have other names, or do you stand behind your friend's choice?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 1) {
 "I think Peter would make a fine king."

"A remarkable suggestion," the king of the underground says.

"Really?" I ask, catching the sudden enthusiasm in his tone.

"Naturally," the king says. "Of the three commanders, Peter commands the deepest loyalty from the rank and file. He has led the rebellion's major sorties from the front, making his face far more recognizable to the common folk of Ollendor than mine. If his candidacy puts your group at ease, I am entirely willing to concede the point. Are there other names to consider, or does everyone agree?"

"I would like to nominate Doctor Reidzek," Daren says. Leila's father jerks his head toward him, eyes wide with alarm.

"Another fine candidate," the king tells Daren, his gaze flicking to Reidzek to take measure of the man. "Doctor Reidzek has earned immense respect in the short time he has spent among us. He would draw a considerable share of the vote if he chose to stand. What do you say, Doctor? Shall I put your name on the ballot?"

"I... must decline," Reidzek says, raising both hands. "Forgive me, Daren, but the crown is too heavy a burden for a simple craftsman. I threw my lot in with this rebellion to mend what was broken in the city, not to govern an entire realm. I hope that does not disappoint you."

"No, not at all," Daren says, stepping back. "It is your choice. I only meant to put the thought forward..."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 2) {
 "In that case, my vote goes for Breynan."

"Breynan?" The king rubs his chin. "What leads you to favor him over the alternatives?"

"He is one of the three rebel captains, and he keeps a level head," I say. "Leading the movement gives him the recognition he needs for the citizens to accept him, and he has the practical sense to guide the kingdom without running it into the dirt."

"All true qualities, yet they apply just as easily to the other two commanders," the king of the underground says. "Anyone who spends five minutes in Breynan's presence knows he lacks charisma. Peter leads through personal warmth, Albert commands through sheer presence, and Breynan is endured because he delivers results. He would make an able administrator, and his intentions are noble enough, but he will never capture the popular vote."

"Are you sure you are not dismissing him because he is the only captain who openly challenges your methods?" I ask.

"Hardly," the king says. "Running against Breynan only makes my path simpler. His odds of taking more votes than me are negligible. If he wishes to enter the race, he is welcome to try. The men would relish the debate. Does your group have any other names, or do you stand with Breynan?"

"I would like to nominate Doctor Reidzek," Daren says. Leila's father jerks his head toward him, eyes wide with alarm.

"Another fine candidate," the king tells Daren, his gaze flicking to Reidzek to take measure of the man. "Doctor Reidzek has earned immense respect in the short time he has spent among us. He would draw a considerable share of the vote if he chose to stand. What do you say, Doctor? Shall I put your name on the ballot?"

"I... must decline," Reidzek says, raising both hands. "Forgive me, Daren, but the crown is too heavy a burden for a simple craftsman. I threw my lot in with this rebellion to mend what was broken in the city, not to govern an entire realm. I hope that does not disappoint you."

"No, not at all," Daren says, stepping back. "It is your choice. I only meant to put the thought forward..."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 3) {
 "If you ask me, I think the king of the underground would be the best pick."

"An ally in your ranks," the king says, offering a slight bow. "My thanks. What about the rest of you? Who would you see on the throne of Ollendor?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 4) {
 "If a trustworthy king is what you're looking for, then Kelrim would be my choice."

"The goblin?" The king of the underground freezes, genuinely caught off guard. "A goblin ruling Ollendor? That is... an unprecedented nomination. We can record it, of course, but... does anyone else have a suggestion?"

"I stand behind Kelrim as well," Daren says firmly. "He has every quality of a worthy ruler."

"Did I hear that correctly?" Kelrim hurries over, his green ears twitching. "Daren, did you just propose me for the throne?"

"Barry put the name forward first, but I agree with him completely," Daren says.

"I am grateful for your faith in me," Kelrim says, looking between the two of us, "but a goblin reigning over a city of humans is unheard of. I am already indebted to the people of Ollendor for letting our kind take shelter within these walls. Claiming the crown feels like abusing their hospitality. Beyond the two of you, who would ever cast a vote for me? I must refuse the nomination."

"If that is your decision, we will respect it," Daren says. "Though I think you sell yourself short, and the people down here along with you."

"Your intentions do you credit," the king tells us, "but Kelrim has chosen wisely. The city will reel from the old monarch's demise. Stoking the fire with such an unconventional candidacy would only court disaster. Now, does anyone else have a proposal?"

"I would like to nominate Doctor Reidzek," Daren says. Leila's father jerks his head toward him, eyes wide with alarm.

"Another fine candidate," the king tells Daren, his gaze flicking to Reidzek to take measure of the man. "Doctor Reidzek has earned immense respect in the short time he has spent among us. He would draw a considerable share of the vote if he chose to stand. What do you say, Doctor? Shall I put your name on the ballot?"

"I... must decline," Reidzek says, raising both hands. "Forgive me, Daren, but the crown is too heavy a burden for a simple craftsman. I threw my lot in with this rebellion to mend what was broken in the city, not to govern an entire realm. I hope that does not disappoint you."

"No, not at all," Daren says, stepping back. "It is your choice. I only meant to put the thought forward..."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 0 || v_b3_ch8_king == 3) {
